S\\)**.\n\n### Step 1: Identify the Dimensions of the Rectangle \\(PQRS\\)\n\nGiven that the area of rectangle \\(PQRS\\) is 24, we need to find its dimensions. Let the length be \\(l\\) and the width be \\(w\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[ l \\times w = 24 \\]\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Coordinates of the Points\n\nAssume:\n- \\(P = (0, w)\\)\n- \\(Q = (l, w)\\)\n- \\(R = (l, 0)\\)\n- \\(S = (0, 0)\\)\n\nGiven that \\(TQ = TR\\) and \\(T\\) is vertically aligned with \\(Q\\) and \\(R\\), it implies that \\(T\\) is the midpoint of the vertical line segment from \\(Q\\) to \\(R\\). Thus, the coordinates of \\(T\\) are:\n\\[ T = (l, \\frac{w}{2}) \\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the Area of Quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\)\n\nThe quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\) can be seen as the rectangle \\(PQRS\\) minus the area of triangle \\(PQT\\).\n\nThe area of triangle \\(PQT\\) can be calculated as follows:\n- The base of triangle \\(PQT\\) is the length \\(l\\).\n- The height of triangle \\(PQT\\) is \\(\\frac{w}{2}\\) (since \\(T\\) is at \\(\\frac{w}{2}\\)).\n\nThe area of triangle \\(PQT\\) is:\n\\[ \\text{Area of } \\triangle PQT = \\frac{1}{2} \\times l \\times \\frac{w}{2} = \\frac{lw}{4} \\]\n\nSince the area of rectangle \\(PQRS\\) is 24, the area of quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\) is:\n\\[ \\text{Area of } PTRS = \\text{Area of } PQRS - \\text{Area of } \\triangle PQT \\]\n\\[ \\text{Area of } PTRS = 24 - \\frac{lw}{4} \\]\n\nGiven that \\(lw = 24\\):\n\\[ \\text{Area of } PTRS = 24 - \\frac{24}{4} = 24 - 6 = 18 \\]\n\nThus, the area of quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{18}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
